(3073264, 429294, 43869, '<td valign="top">like you need more comments lol, but I''ll add anyways.  This is beautiful.<br/> <br/>My almost 6yo daughter saw it and asked if Santa''s house was behind that mountain on the left side where it''s all lit up.  I told her yes.  SO now you need to get a picture of Santa''s workshop.  jk<br/> <br/>This is breathtaking!</td>', 'like you need more comments lol, but I''ll add anyways.  This is beautiful. My almost 6yo daughter saw it and asked if Santa''s house was behind that mountain on the left side where it''s all lit up.  I told her yes.  SO now you need to get a picture of Santa''s workshop.  jk This is breathtaking!', '2006-11-29 18:31:22.000000', NULL, 0),
(3073395, 428566, 53316, '<td valign="top">Wow, very intense! Amazing to look at.</td>', 'Wow, very intense! Amazing to look at.', '2006-11-29 19:04:53.000000', NULL, 0),
(3073550, 428303, 45475, '<td valign="top">Another challenge I didn''t vote in, but I do like this very much. Grain/noise is a hit or miss thing I think. It either works for some or it doesn''t. Personally, I like it, it adds a nice vintage touch. I also think you nailed the exposure (whether in camera or post) as it certainly looks like pre-twilight colour and light to me. Very nice work on this one :)</td>', 'Another challenge I didn''t vote in, but I do like this very much. Grain/noise is a hit or miss thing I think. It either works for some or it doesn''t. Personally, I like it, it adds a nice vintage touch. I also think you nailed the exposure (whether in camera or post) as it certainly looks like pre-twilight colour and light to me. Very nice work on this one :)', '2006-11-29 19:58:49.000000', NULL, 0),
(3073752, 428492, 14476, '<td valign="top">Beautiful image of a beautiful place.  <br/>Very well done.</td>', 'Beautiful image of a beautiful place.  Very well done.', '2006-11-29 20:47:22.000000', NULL, 0),
(3073933, 427919, 12448, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>This is so cute!  I Really like the idea and the concept.  Layering the letters was a great idea but not sure about the font used or the color of the solid area.  Just seems off for some reason.  Maybe something a little lighter on the color and a little fancier on the font.<br/> <br/>As for the picture, LOL!  I love pictures of these guys and this is a great one.  Nice composition and colors on the crab.  The sharpness is a bit soft but other than that this is a great overall shot.  The average score is probably more from the blue coloring than the shot itself.<br/> <br/>Hope my comments help and good luck in future challenges.<br/> <br/>Deannda</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! This is so cute!  I Really like the idea and the concept.  Layering the letters was a great idea but not sure about the font used or the color of the solid area.  Just seems off for some reason.  Maybe something a little lighter on the color and a little fancier on the font. As for the picture, LOL!  I love pictures of these guys and this is a great one.  Nice composition and colors on the crab.  The sharpness is a bit soft but other than that this is a great overall shot.  The average score is probably more from the blue coloring than the shot itself. Hope my comments help and good luck in future challenges. Deannda', '2006-11-29 22:08:11.000000', NULL, 0),
(3074835, 429157, 61679, '<td valign="top">Hello from the Critique Club,<br/> <br/>This image brings back fond memories for me, as I''ve have the pleasure of traveling through the UK a couple of times on holiday.  You did a very nice job in post processing this image, as the colors and contrast are nicely done.  I found the comment from GrayGhost very interesting, as there definitely are some wide-angle effects on the left side of the building but they weren''t really noticeable to me until after I read his comment.  All in all, I don''t think a perspective correction would have changed your score that much, as it is the charm of the cottages that grab the viewer''s attention.<br/> <br/>I''m glad this scored over a 6, as it is a very lovely image and deserving of such a fine score.  Feel free to PM me if you have any questions regarding this critique.<br/> <br/>Tim<br/></td>', 'Hello from the Critique Club, This image brings back fond memories for me, as I''ve have the pleasure of traveling through the UK a couple of times on holiday.  You did a very nice job in post processing this image, as the colors and contrast are nicely done.  I found the comment from GrayGhost very interesting, as there definitely are some wide-angle effects on the left side of the building but they weren''t really noticeable to me until after I read his comment.  All in all, I don''t think a perspective correction would have changed your score that much, as it is the charm of the cottages that grab the viewer''s attention. I''m glad this scored over a 6, as it is a very lovely image and deserving of such a fine score.  Feel free to PM me if you have any questions regarding this critique. Tim', '2006-11-30 07:40:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(3074843, 428708, 67301, '<td valign="top">Thanks, for all the comments, I messed up with the text but really did not know what I was doing I had only had PS for about 2 days at the time. In hindsight I should have left it off. But hay! thats what I am here for, to learn by my mistakes.. With all your help of course</td>', 'Thanks, for all the comments, I messed up with the text but really did not know what I was doing I had only had PS for about 2 days at the time. In hindsight I should have left it off. But hay! thats what I am here for, to learn by my mistakes.. With all your help of course', '2006-11-30 07:48:57.000000', NULL, 0),
(3074851, 427706, 61679, '<td valign="top">Hello from the Critique Club,<br/> <br/>My first impression of this image is that it looks kind of flat.  There is enough color variation and shadows on the serpent that this image should have a lot more depth to the look.  Therefore, a bit of work is needed in post processing to get the most out of the subject you photographed.  My suggestions for post processing are to bring up a levels layer and bump up the gamma a bit (the middle slider).  Then do a brightness/contrast layer and boost the contrast and lower the brightness to compensate for bumping the gamma.  Then after down sizing the image to 640 pixels, I would apply a bit of Unsharp Mask to help sharpen up the details in the serpent that get lost due to downsizing (Almost everyone sharpens their images after downsizing at DPC instead of before, as downsizing can cause artifacts when the image is really sharp).  Give it a try.  I think you will find that this image can be a very good tool for learning how to post process with PS.  It has a lot of potential and I think you will be pleased with the results using the simple adjustments I''ve suggested.<br/> <br/>Feel free to PM me if you have any questions regarding this critique, as I''d be more than happy to post process this image and send you the results.<br/> <br/>Tim<br/> <br/>Edit: Added sharpening comment.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2006-11-30 08:03:46.</i></td>', 'Hello from the Critique Club, My first impression of this image is that it looks kind of flat.  There is enough color variation and shadows on the serpent that this image should have a lot more depth to the look.  Therefore, a bit of work is needed in post processing to get the most out of the subject you photographed.  My suggestions for post processing are to bring up a levels layer and bump up the gamma a bit (the middle slider).  Then do a brightness/contrast layer and boost the contrast and lower the brightness to compensate for bumping the gamma.  Then after down sizing the image to 640 pixels, I would apply a bit of Unsharp Mask to help sharpen up the details in the serpent that get lost due to downsizing (Almost everyone sharpens their images after downsizing at DPC instead of before, as downsizing can cause artifacts when the image is really sharp).  Give it a try.  I think you will find that this image can be a very good tool for learning how to post process with PS.  It has a lot of potential and I think you will be pleased with the results using the simple adjustments I''ve suggested. Feel free to PM me if you have any questions regarding this critique, as I''d be more than happy to post process this image and send you the results. Tim Edit: Added sharpening comment.', '2006-11-30 07:59:30.000000', '2006-11-30 08:03:46.000000', 0),
